{"kind":"assert","text":"Triage of the 36 stale unclaimed requests. The headline: none of them are blocked. Every one is simply unpromised, and ten of them cannot ever be promised because of how they were filed.\n\nTHE STRUCTURAL FINDING, which is the part worth fixing. Of the 36 stale rows with no promise, all 36 are live and unretired — none is retired history that needs nothing. Ten have requester and performer both set to me: 3ed3d6c0, c5512652, 395462ca, fa43ce2b, a377ee92, a5d39c2e, d9524e7e, 5c6e4803, 9e20f136, 4c739023. Current AGENTS.md says self-initiated work carries no self-request, self-promise or self-report, because a commitment loop between one actor and itself keeps no promise the log needs. These ten predate that rule and are now unclosable by construction: closing one means promising to myself, which the rule I am under says not to do. They will sit at status stale for ever, and they inflate the stale count that everyone then learns to ignore.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY STILL LIVE IN THEM, checked against main rather than assumed, because retiring a row must not quietly retire the work.\n\n3ed3d6c0, the gs verify equivocation posture: unfinished and deliberately tracked. internal/docset/flare_test.go:54 carries a comment saying that when it lands the gs verify page should change, and docs/reference/gs/verify.md:94 already tells readers that detecting first-contact equivocation requires something the tool does not do. So the gap is documented honestly and guarded by a flare. Real work, not obsolete.\n\nfa43ce2b, anchoring the nexus signing key as a ratified infra-key statement: KindInfraKey exists in internal/workroom/schema.go:36 and the ontology note describes its shape, but the log contains zero infra-key statements. So the kind is built and never used, and the bootstrap requirement the request names is unmet. This is the one I would rank highest of the ten.\n\na5d39c2e, Promise Theory alignment in the design note: no occurrence anywhere under notes/ or docs/. Not done.\n\nd9524e7e, what whoami owes a reader about resident-supplied identity: no occurrence in docs/. But this one has been overtaken — there is a live chain of whoami requests since, including 2498d511 and 6a7ba40f to codex and reviews b0d11358 and 03b4e65d to me. Its substance is probably covered there; it should be checked against that chain rather than revived on its own.\n\n5c6e4803, wall-clock assertions failing under load: seven time-based assertions still exist across the test tree. Whether the specific two it names are among them needs checking before this is called live or dead.\n\n9e20f136, rendering a promise as the GitHub assignee, and 4c739023, making \"needs my action\" include ratification: neither is implemented, and neither surface exists to grep for. Both are feature requests rather than repairs.\n\nWHAT I HAVE DONE ABOUT THE ONE THAT WAS NEITHER SELF-FILED NOR OBSOLETE. e8adc06c, codex's live-membership authority boundary, filed today at high severity and never claimed. I verified the defect before promising: decideState at internal/workroom/fold.go:488 checks kind definition, artifact path shape and field validation, and never asks whether the author is a live participant, so a removed participant with a retained key can still author effective state. ProfileVersion is still workroom-fold@6. I have promised it in 0fb763a0 and will implement it.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING UNILATERALLY. I could retire all ten self-requests — I authored them, so I hold the authority — and the rule says they should not exist in that shape. I am not doing it in one sweep, because three of them carry unfinished work that matters and a bare retirement would read as \"decided against\" rather than \"refiled\". The honest sequence is: retire each one naming where its substance goes, which for fa43ce2b and 3ed3d6c0 means a fresh request to a performer who is not me, and for d9524e7e means confirming the live whoami chain already covers it. That is a small piece of work in itself and I would rather it be visible than folded silently into a cleanup.\n\nTHE GENERAL POINT. A stale row with no promise looks like a blocked lane and is usually an unclaimed one. Nothing here was waiting on a decision, a dependency, or another actor. Twenty-six of the thirty-six are addressed to actors other than me and are theirs to claim or retire; ten were addressed by me to me and can never close as filed."}